## Why This Is Asked
Tests edge routing with simple feature flag logic using standard nginx capabilities, keeping it beginner-friendly while still requiring practical setup.

## Key Concepts
- nginx upstreams and map for header-driven routing
- TLS termination in docker-compose
- cross-DC networking and service naming

## Code Example
```nginx
http {
  upstream api-old { server api-old:8080; }
  upstream api-new { server api-new:8080; }
  map $http_x_feature_flag_alpha $target { default api-old; 1 api-new; }
  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    ssl_certificate /certs/server.crt; ssl_certificate_key /certs/server.key;
    location / { proxy_pass http://$target; }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test header-driven routing in CI?
- How would you add health checks and a safe rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests secure, rotating certs in Swarm with Vault, handling cert storage (secret vs volume), and canary semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Swarm multi-node with TLS and mTLS
- Vault PKI for dynamic certs
- Certs via writable volume + sidecar refresh
- Canary rollout with health checks and rollback
- Nginx TLS termination and service-to-service mTLS

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple cert refresh watcher from Vault to a mounted volume
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const fs = require('fs');
async function refresh() {
  const res = await fetch('https://vault.example/v1/pki/issue/api', { headers: { 'X-Vault-Token': process.env.VAULT_TOKEN } });
  const cert = await res.text();
  fs.writeFileSync('/certs/api.crt', cert);
}
setInterval(refresh, 5 * 60 * 1000);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cert rotation does not break existing connections?
- How would you monitor Vault token renewal and automatic revocation?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical ability to extend a docker-dca setup with a compliant, low-latency data-redaction path.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config-driven per-tenant policies
- Sidecar pattern and in-memory caching
- Streaming JSON redaction without full buffering
- Nginx-edge integration and TLS context

## Code Example
```go
package main
import (
  "encoding/json"
  "fmt"
)
func redact(m map[string]interface{}, fields map[string]bool){
  for k,v := range m {
    if fields[k] { m[k] = "***REDACTED***"; continue }
    if mm, ok := v.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(mm, fields) }
    if arr, ok := v.([]interface{}); ok {
      for _, it := range arr { if sub, ok := it.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(sub, fields) } }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test nested JSON redaction with large bodies?
- How would you monitor redaction performance without affecting latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design per-tenant QoS with dynamic config in a multi-DC setup.

## Key Concepts
- Traefik RateLimit middleware, Redis store, per-tenant quotas, Docker Config
- TLS termination, hot-reload without restart, cross-DC coordination
- Request tagging with X-Tenant and validation of isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo middleware sketch: extract tenant and enforce quota from Redis
async function enforceTenantRate(req, res, next){
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'];
  const quota = await redisGetQuota(tenant);
  if (quota.allow(req)) return next();
  res.status(429).send('Too Many Requests');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you audit cross-DC quota drift and recover from Redis replication lag?
- How would you test hot-reload behavior under config churn?
